# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

Grants an existing role directly to a principal. `role_name` accepts preset roles
(`admin`, `builder`, `explorer`) and custom roles. Use `grantee_type = "user"`
for both people and service accounts, or `grantee_type = "role"` for inheritance.
The SQL identity in `MOTHERDUCK_TOKEN` (or provider `token`) must have permission
to assign roles. Configuring only `admin_token` does not authorize SQL grants.

## Example Usage

### Grant a preset or existing custom role

Use the existing role's name without declaring a `motherduck_role` resource:

```terraform
resource "motherduck_role_grant" "builder" {
  role_name    = "builder"
  grantee_name = "svc_analytics_reader"
  grantee_type = "user"
}
```

The same configuration works with an existing custom role name. The grantee must
already exist. For an account or role created in the same configuration, reference
its resource attribute so Terraform orders creation and destruction correctly.

### Create a custom role, give it platform permissions, and assign it

{{tffile "examples/resources/motherduck_role_grant/resource.tf"}}

Here `analytics_readers` inherits Explorer permissions. Assigning it to the service
account gives the account those inherited permissions. Share data access is a
separate [motherduck_share_grant](share_grant.md) resource.

## Lifecycle

This resource owns one **direct** grant. Inherited membership alone does not
satisfy it. If the direct grant is revoked outside Terraform, the next apply
restores it. Destroy revokes that grant without deleting either role or principal.
Permissions received through other grants remain in effect.

### Roles in secure views

A [secure view](share_secure_view.md) can check role membership with
`is_role_in_session('role_name')` to mask a column for consumers outside the
role. Grants and revokes take up to about a minute to
reach `is_role_in_session()`, in open sessions as well as new ones, so a revoked
role can still unmask a column briefly.

### Organizations that manage roles through SCIM

An organization can make its identity provider the source of truth for user role
assignment. MotherDuck then refuses role grants and revokes for people, and the
provider reports the refusal with the reason. Grants to service accounts and to
roles keep working. If destroy is refused, MotherDuck keeps the grant and Terraform
keeps it in state. Manage the person's roles in the identity provider, or run
`terraform state rm` on the resource to stop managing the grant without revoking it.

## Import

Import a direct grant using `<role_name>/<grantee_type>/<grantee_name>`:

{{codefile "shell" "examples/resources/motherduck_role_grant/import.sh"}}

Preset-role grants use the same format, for example
`builder/user/svc_analytics_reader`. Inheritance uses `explorer/role/analytics_readers`.
Import existing direct grants before applying if Terraform should own them.

See MotherDuck's [GRANT ROLE](https://motherduck.com/docs/sql-reference/motherduck-sql-reference/access-control/grant-role/)
reference for role assignment and inheritance.

